Programming in JavaScript<P>
<HR>
<H1>Appendix C - Error messages</H1>
<HR>
When a program goes wrong, an error box appears with the program's name in its title bar, a message saying
what went wrong and, in brackets, the line where it happened. This appendix lists the messages you are most
likely to meet, exactly as they appear, with what they usually mean. In the messages below, <I>x</I> stands
for whatever name or character your program used.<P>
There are two kinds of error:<P>
<UL>
<LI><B>Mistakes in the writing</B> (<I>syntax errors</I>) stop JavaScript from understanding the program at
all, so none of it runs. The line given is where JavaScript realised something was wrong, which is sometimes
a line or two after the actual mistake.<P>
<LI><B>Mistakes in what the program does</B> happen while it runs. Everything up to the failing line has
already happened: some output may have been printed, or a window opened. An error in an event handler or a
timer happens later, when that code runs.<P>
</UL>

<A NAME=S1></A>
<H2>Mistakes in the writing</H2>
<DL>
<DT>Unexpected token 'x'
<DD>JavaScript found a character or word where it doesn't make sense - often one that is doubled or
missing its partner: <CODE>a +* 2</CODE>, <CODE>if (a = 2 {</CODE>. Look at the line given, and the one
before it.<P>
<DT>Invalid or unexpected token
<DD>Usually a string with a missing quote at one end: <CODE>print('Hello);</CODE>. Also caused by a
character JavaScript doesn't recognise at all.<P>
<DT>missing ) after argument list
<DD>A function call is missing its closing bracket, or something inside the brackets is wrong - often a
missing <CODE>+</CODE> or comma between two values: <CODE>print('Total' total)</CODE>.<P>
<DT>Unexpected end of input
<DD>JavaScript reached the end of the program while it was still expecting something - nearly always a
missing closing curly bracket <CODE>}</CODE>. The line given is the last line of the program, so it won't
point at the mistake: check that every <CODE>{</CODE> has a matching <CODE>}</CODE>. Keeping the indentation tidy makes
this much easier.<P>
<DT>Identifier 'x' has already been declared
<DD>The same name has been declared twice with <CODE>let</CODE> or <CODE>const</CODE> in the same part of
the program. To change a variable's value, leave out <CODE>let</CODE> the second time:
<CODE>score = 2;</CODE>.<P>
<DT>await is only valid in async functions and the top level bodies of modules
<DD><CODE>await</CODE> has been used inside a function that isn't marked <CODE>async</CODE>. Put
<CODE>async</CODE> in front of the function: <CODE>async function load() { ... }</CODE>, or
<CODE>async (ev) =&gt; { ... }</CODE> for an arrow function.<P>
</DL>

<A NAME=S2></A>
<H2>Mistakes while running</H2>
<DL>
<DT>x is not defined
<DD>JavaScript doesn't know anything called <I>x</I>. Usually a typing mistake - <CODE>prnt</CODE> for
<CODE>print</CODE>, <CODE>totl</CODE> for <CODE>total</CODE> - or the wrong capitals (<CODE>Score</CODE> and
<CODE>score</CODE> are different). It can also mean a variable was declared inside a function or a
<CODE>{ }</CODE> block and is being used outside it, or, in a module, that a name wasn't imported.<P>
<DT>Cannot access 'x' before initialization
<DD><I>x</I> is declared with <CODE>let</CODE> or <CODE>const</CODE>, but is being used on a line before the
declaration. Move the declaration up.<P>
<DT>Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'x')
<DD>The program asked for <CODE>something.x</CODE>, but <I>something</I> has no value. Common causes: an
array index past the end (<CODE>list[10]</CODE> of a list of three), a misspelt property name
(<CODE>game.snek[0]</CODE>), a variable that was declared but never given a value, or a function that
doesn't <CODE>return</CODE> anything. <I>... of null</I> means the same, with <CODE>null</CODE> instead.<P>
<DT>x is not a function
<DD>The program used <I>x</I> with brackets after it, as if it were a function, but it isn't one: a
misspelt function or method name (<CODE>list.psh(4)</CODE>), a method the value doesn't have (strings have no
<CODE>push</CODE>), or a variable that has the same name as a function.<P>
<DT>Assignment to constant variable.
<DD>The program gives a new value to a variable declared with <CODE>const</CODE>. Declare it with
<CODE>let</CODE> instead if its value needs to change.<P>
<DT>x is not iterable
<DD><CODE>for (const item of x)</CODE> was used with something that isn't a list, such as a number, or
<CODE>undefined</CODE>.<P>
<DT>Maximum call stack size exceeded
<DD>A function calls itself (perhaps through other functions) without ever stopping.<P>
<DT>Unexpected token 'x', "..." is not valid JSON
<DD>Also: <I>Expected property name or '}' in JSON</I>. <CODE>JSON.parse</CODE> was given text that isn't
JSON: the file isn't the kind you expected, or was damaged. Chapter 12 shows how to cope, with
<CODE>try ... catch</CODE> (see also appendix A).<P>
<DT>File 'x' not found
<DD>A RISC OS error from the filing system: there is no file of that name. Check the spelling and the full
name, and use <CODE>vfs.exists</CODE> first if the file might not be there. Other filing system errors, such
as <I>Directory 'x' not found</I> and <I>'x' is locked</I>, are the same as RISC OS gives anywhere else.<P>
</DL>

<A NAME=S3></A>
<H2>Errors from modules</H2>
<DL>
<DT>x doesn't export a start function (export default function start(task, ctx) { ... })
<DD>The program is a module - it uses <CODE>import</CODE> or <CODE>export</CODE> - but has no start function
for <CODE>JSRun</CODE> to call. Add <CODE>export default function start(task, ctx) { ... }</CODE> and put the
program in it.<P>
<DT>Can't find './x' (imported by a program in ...)
<DD>An <CODE>import</CODE> names a file that isn't in the directory given. Check the spelling, and that the
file is where you think: <CODE>'./Board'</CODE> means a file called <CODE>Board</CODE> in the same directory
as the file doing the importing.<P>
<DT>The requested module '...' does not provide an export named 'x'
<DD>An <CODE>import { x }</CODE> asks for something the other module doesn't export - misspelt, or missing
the word <CODE>export</CODE> in front of it. The module named is the file being imported from, and the
line given is the line of the <CODE>import</CODE>.<P>
</DL>

<A NAME=S4></A>
<H2>When a program won't stop</H2>
A loop whose condition never becomes false - <CODE>while (true) { }</CODE> with no <CODE>break</CODE>, or a
<CODE>for</CODE> loop counting the wrong way - keeps the computer busy for ever. Because the whole desktop runs
inside your web browser, everything stops responding: the pointer moves, but nothing else happens.<P>
If this happens, reload the page in your web browser. The desktop starts again. Anything saved on the hard
disc is kept, so the program itself is still there to be corrected; anything that wasn't saved is lost.
Save your program before running it, and you won't lose any work.<P>
A program that repeats something slowly - with <CODE>task.every</CODE>, for example - never has this problem:
between steps the desktop carries on as normal, and you can stop the program with the Task Manager: click
Select on the Acorn icon at the right of the icon bar to open its window, click Menu over the program's name
and choose <I>Task '...' &gt; Quit</I>.<P>

